## Configuration, custody and credit

Require configured original wallet, canonical economic origin, fixed source,
original eligible native19-share quantity, evidence identity and explicitly
known original ETH/DTF components. Operational originals remain inputs, not
values inferred from holdings. Synthetic fixture amounts must remain labeled.
Compute C0 once through the existing frozen quote, never live vault/SY rates.

Owner Stage1 full/idempotent replacement creates no bond. Cross-origin
guards work in both directions against independent compensation, both NFT
families, WD, WY, W-SD and direct vault shares. Do not approve opaque aliases
as economically disjoint or assign indirect weight rights a second backing
award. No on-demand configuration or transfer-history oracle.

Stage2 original wallet with matching record must actually approve and
surrender q native shares. Measure exact wallet debit and proxy receipt,
book only the received q, consume remaining C/Q with full-precision floor
and exact terminal residue, and create one exact-funded fresh90-day cliff
bond atomically. Missing record, wrong wallet/source, invalid quantities,
zero credit, min/deadline, custody or child-funding failure leaves no completed
custody/credit/supply/bond effect. Unsolicited shares are not admitted.

## Staged recovery and protected accounting

Owner-selected booked shares redeem with `redeem(q,MIG,MIG)` into fixed MIG
custody. Burn exactly the selected shares and reconcile measured SY output,
returned amount, positive output and minimum. Use actual source rounding
and units; the old observed 2769125838408621163 shares ->276912584 SY is
historical evidence, not a universal price or future hardcoded result.

Book measured SY once into the existing WY recovery inventory, then reuse
actual WY-to-WS-gons-to-WD-to-DTF/fixed-donation stages. No second personal
credit or ordinary bond purchase. Protect prior surrendered WY, booked
NFT/WD/W-SD inventory, unbooked shares/SY/gons/DTF, donation/residual budgets
and funded bond backing. Failed selected batches roll back completely;
later failed transactions do not erase earlier committed recovery.

